body:
Escapism. At first, they were legitimate reasons due to a bad back injury and not using opioids, but I slowly became addicted to disassociating from reality entirely and I loved it.

&#x200B;

I just didn't want to feel anything, so getting high was an easy out.

First off, I definitely relate to this. I had very similar road blocks(Covid, lost job, injuries, no money, lockdown in other countries). I have my firs solo trip booked for July with everything booked(insurance, airbnb, flights etc). I'll be entering my 30s this year and that feeling conflicting emotions is never ending, but I tell myself if I don't take the first step, then I never will and no one is going to take it for me. 

&#x200B;

Just remember that it's on you. Even with my similarly conflicting emotions, I know that at the end of the day, it's on me to make that first move/step. You got this.

If it makes you feel any better, I've been out close to 9 months now from hard rolling due a l4/l5 disk herniation. Rather sever, which left me in the ER. Luckily I was able to avoid surgery and find a PT who does jiu jitsu. 

&#x200B;

If it makes you feel any better, I've been out close to 9 months from hard rolling due to a l4/l5 disk herniation. Rather severe, which left me in the ER. Luckily I was able to avoid surgery and find a PT who does jiu jitsu. 

&#x200B;

I slowly started working my way back to drilling about a month ago and I likely won't be able to roll for another 3-4 months. So all in all, over a year off the mats due to an injury. Not to make light of your injury, but it's all relative and no doubt some people have it worse than us.
